# Normal handling for everything; the RTL and scripts are edited by humans on
# both platforms.
#
# This catch-all MUST come first. In gitattributes the *last* matching pattern
# wins, so a broad `* text=auto` written below a narrow rule silently overrides
# it -- which is what happened here: the vectors carried `-text` on the line
# above this one and `git check-attr` still reported `text: auto` for them.
* text=auto

# The committed vectors are compared byte for byte against freshly generated
# ones by scripts/check_vectors.py. If git normalised their line endings on
# checkout, that comparison would fail on a clean tree and the failure would
# read as a stale-model error -- the exact confusion the check exists to avoid.
#
# That is not hypothetical. With this rule ordered *before* the catch-all it
# never took effect, so on any machine with core.autocrlf=true (the Git for
# Windows default) checkout rewrote all 48 vector files to CRLF while the model
# kept emitting LF, and `make vectors-check` failed every one of them with a
# message accusing the golden model of having drifted. Content was identical in
# all 48; only the line endings differed.
model/vectors/** -text
